WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.400
So, Boolean logic and Python, you might have heard this expression truthiness.

00:08.400 --> 00:14.560
Truthiness is figuring out what expressions evaluate to true or false in Python,

00:14.560 --> 00:17.920
and that's going to help us control the flow of our program.

00:20.880 --> 00:24.720
Now, I have a little handy cheat sheet for you here at the top.

00:25.440 --> 00:29.920
With integers, truthiness works a little bit differently than you might expect.

00:31.520 --> 00:38.480
The truthiness of integers 0 is false, all other numbers that have a value are true,

00:38.480 --> 00:40.720
including negative numbers.

00:42.720 --> 00:47.200
For container types, like the list, the tuple, the set, the dictionary,

00:47.920 --> 00:54.640
empty containers evaluate to false containers with items in them evaluate to true.

00:55.520 --> 00:59.680
Now, the none or null type is always false.

01:01.520 --> 01:08.480
There are also keyword arguments of true with a capital T and false with a capital F.

01:10.560 --> 01:16.960
Now, sometimes truthiness is pretty obvious.

01:17.680 --> 01:19.680
Is this true or false?

01:21.920 --> 01:22.960
It's true, right?

01:24.320 --> 01:26.880
I tried it the reverse way, that's false.

01:29.280 --> 01:38.000
You can always figure out what truthiness your expression will have by calling Boo on it,

01:38.800 --> 01:45.280
and Boo maps to the constructor for the built-in true or false.

01:46.080 --> 01:52.080
So, I can call Boo with one, which is true, Boo with zero, which is false.

01:52.480 --> 01:56.320
But I can also pass in some complex expressions here,

01:57.040 --> 02:00.880
and it will let me know what the truthiness value of them is.

02:03.600 --> 02:09.600
So, calling Boo on an empty list, false, putting some items into it is true.

02:09.760 --> 02:16.880
Boo of none type is always false.

02:20.000 --> 02:26.880
You can compare different items in Python with less than greater than less than or equals.

02:27.760 --> 02:32.160
But there's also the double equals and the not equals.

02:35.280 --> 02:38.160
So, double equals is quality.

02:39.920 --> 02:41.840
And this is not equals.

02:46.640 --> 02:51.760
Equality for different container types is a little bit interesting.

02:54.240 --> 02:59.520
So, equality for lists will check for the items in the list.

03:00.160 --> 03:02.160
So, let's say I have list one.

03:02.960 --> 03:12.720
And list two, if I call list one equals list two, I'm checking for equality of the

03:12.720 --> 03:19.120
contents, and I'm not asking is this the same exact list.

03:20.720 --> 03:27.520
If you do want to check for, hey, are these lists pointing at the same variable?

03:28.480 --> 03:34.960
That is with, you're going to use a different keyword called is the checks for identity.

03:42.320 --> 03:45.440
Basically, asking to these points the same place in memory.

03:52.640 --> 03:55.360
They don't, they're stored at different variables.

03:56.000 --> 04:03.520
So, this is a little bit of a more advanced topic, but the reason that I'm introducing it

04:06.160 --> 04:15.920
is because we have a lot of keywords in Python that we've seen, like true and false and none.

04:16.640 --> 04:26.000
In order to check for equality with these keywords, we want to use the is keyword for to check for

04:26.000 --> 04:31.760
identity, do these things point to the same place in memory and not with equality.

04:32.640 --> 04:38.400
This is especially handy with none because

04:39.200 --> 04:44.000
a empty list can evaluate to false.

04:45.280 --> 04:53.680
So, if we want to differentiate between none and an empty list, we need to use the is keyword.

04:55.120 --> 05:02.240
And we can say is x none. Oops. Sorry, x is none, and that is true.

05:02.880 --> 05:12.880
If I ask, empty list is none, that is false. So, if you need to differentiate between the

05:12.880 --> 05:20.160
truthiness of a none value or something that has a false truthiness, like an empty list or zero,

05:20.160 --> 05:22.160
you're going to need to use that is keyword.

05:22.400 --> 05:38.400
And same for true and false. So, don't do a equals true. If you want to know explicitly,

05:38.400 --> 05:48.720
you can say a is true. Oops. I would need to define that and that will say true.

05:49.680 --> 05:53.040
So, the difference between equality and identity.

05:54.880 --> 06:01.040
There's also the and or and not statements in Python.

06:02.160 --> 06:10.240
One of the great things about Python is that these statements are just English keywords.

06:13.840 --> 06:18.480
And it kind of spells out like you would type a sentence for those of you who might come from Java,

